# Git commits

After each logical change, create a commit immediately. Do not wait for the
user to ask. Do not batch unrelated files into one commit.

## Size

One concern per commit. If a subject would need `;` or the word `and`,
split into several commits instead. Do not use `;` or `and` in the subject
or the body.

Keep Conventional Commits: `<type>(<scope>): <description>` in lowercase,
imperative, no trailing period. Prefer no body. If a body is needed, do not
use `;` or `and` there either.

```text
# BAD
feat(nvim): prefer uv python for mason and host
docs: rewrite readme; document install

# GOOD
feat(nvim): point mason at uv python
feat(nvim): set python3_host_prog from uv
docs: rewrite readme for current stack
```

## Amend

`git commit --amend` is allowed only when all of these hold:

- The commit has not been pushed to a remote
- `HEAD` was created in this session
- The follow-up is the same logical change (typo, missed file, hook edit)

If a commit was rejected by a hook, fix it in a **new** commit. Do not amend.
Do not skip hooks. Do not amend commits you did not create.
